    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 022045 CCA\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n129 PM PDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Drier, cooler, and breezy conditions expected through the work week.\n  \n\n- Storm chances return this weekend with higher rain potential\n  from monsoonal push and possible remnants of Tropical Storm Marie.\n  \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nMostly clear skies and a cooling trend continues today with highs \naround 5 degrees below average. Aside from gusty winds (with peak \ngusts around 30mph) the forecast remains fairly calm and dry with \ndew points falling to around 10 degrees and RH being less that 10 \npercent. Thursday and Friday will continue the relatively cool \nconditions (yes, highs in the 90s are relatively cool in the valley \nthis time of year) with monsoonal influence beginning to add some \nslight improvement to humidities into the area. Winds will increase \nover the next few days with higher terrain and favored gaps beginning \nto see gusts above 40 mph Thursday and Friday afternoon.\n\nThe increasing winds and building moisture look to fuel storm \nchances over the weekend. Some mid to longer term models also show a \nportion of the remnants from Hurricane Marie adding tropical moisture \nto the mix over the weekend. These ingredients will combine to \noffer strong chances for fairly widespread showers and storms on \nSaturday with increased chances on Sunday as of the most recent \nforecast updates. While there is still some changes possible for \nthis portion of the forecast, you may still want to prepare for \nhazardous weather for the holiday weekend, especially Sunday. Storms \nwill offer plenty of lightning, rain, and strong gusts. Shower \nchances will be more widespread and with increased the PWATs over \nthe weekend, could provide heavy rainfall, leading to flood risks. \nAdding to the risk of flooding will be the potential for storm and \nshower tracks to train over portions of the region, leading to \npersistent rain fall. Rain chances reduce into Labor Day, but linger \ninto the next work week\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nExpect S-SW winds through at least sunset with gusts of 25-30 kts. S-\nSW winds will likely remain elevated into the evening at 10-15 kts, \nbut confidence is lower if gusts will persist into the overnight \nhours. The gust S-SW winds will re-develop by mid morning on \nThursday with gusts of 25-30 kts once again through the afternoon. \nTemperatures remain below 100 degrees through the forecast period. \nNo cig or vis concerns. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Generally S-SW winds \nacross the area through the forecast period with the exception near \nKBIH where winds will be more diurnal in nature. Wind gusts of 20-30 \nkts will persist in most locations through sunset. Lighter winds \novernight with S-SW winds will increase by mid to late morning on \nThursday with gusts of 20-30 kts. No operationally significant cloud \ncover though the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Czyzyk\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
